.TH MU2EDAQ-POWER-RECOVERY 7 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Overview"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq-power-recovery \- overview of the Mu2e DAQ power-outage recovery tools
.SH DESCRIPTION
A set of tools for recovering the Mu2e DAQ computing centres from a planned or
unplanned power outage, driven from a workstation outside the DAQ networks.
.PP
Three computing centres are covered:
.BR MC-2 ,
the primary DAQ room;
.BR MC-1 ;
and the
.B Teststand
in the Helen Edwards Engineering Center. Each has up to three local networks
\(em lab, data and IPMI \(em with different CIDRs per site, recorded in
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5).
.SH HOW THE TOOLS REACH THE CLUSTER
.TP
.B SSH
Direct to the gateways; everything else through one with
.BR ssh 's
.B \-J
ProxyJump. Authentication is Kerberos/GSSAPI with delegation, so the ticket
that opened the gateway session authenticates the second hop. There is no SSH
password path; a password, when one is asked for at all, is for
.BR kinit (1).
A changed host key is classified separately from an authentication failure and
stops the attempt at once rather than advancing to the next credential \(em no
credential can get past it. After an outage the usual cause is a reimaged node,
but the alternative reading is an intercepted connection, so the new
fingerprint has to be verified out of band, with
.B ssh\-keyscan
run from a gateway, before
.I known_hosts
is touched.
.TP
.B IPMI
.BR ipmitool (1)
is run
.I on a gateway,
never locally: the IPMI subnets are private and not routable from off site.
This is also what the requirements ask for \(em IPMI commands issued from
gateway01 or gateway02.
.TP
.B Credentials
No single identity can log in to every node, so each host is tried against an
ordered chain. The operator's own principal is always first, for root sessions
as much as ordinary ones; then the Mu2e service identities \(em
.IR mu2edaq ", " mu2eshift
and the rest \(em whose tickets the
.B mu2edaq-kerberos
package mints from keytabs in Vault. Only an authentication failure advances
the chain; an unreachable host, or a changed host key, stops it. A credential
that works is remembered for that host, but only among the fallbacks: the
operator's own principal is never displaced from the head of the chain.
.IP
Root sessions fall back too. What varies is the ticket, not the login: the root
login stays
.I root
and a node's
.I root/.k5login
may well authorise
.IR mu2edaq .
Set
.I kerberos.root_fallback
false to try the root principal alone. The login is taken from the SSH
configuration and is never derived from the principal \(em
.I mu2edaq
and
.I root
both log in with the operator's personal ticket, and an account named after
the operator's principal does not exist on those hosts.
.TP
.B Credential caches
Tickets are minted into caches private to the run and destroyed when it ends.
On MIT Kerberos that is a file under the run's own temporary directory. On
Heimdal \(em macOS \(em it is an entry in an
.B API:
collection, and each newly minted cache becomes the collection's default, so
minting a service ticket displaces the operator's personal one rather than
destroying it. The run records the default before each mint and restores the
pointer afterwards; the service chain is abandoned only if a restore actually
fails. A run that starts with a service identity already in the default cache
says so and prints the
.BR kswitch / kdestroy
line that puts it right.
.IP
BMC credentials come from HashiCorp Vault at
.I td/scd/experiments/mu2e/ipmi/config
and are delivered to the gateway on standard input, so they appear in no
process table on either host.
.SH THE FOUR PHASES
.TP
.B Phase 0 \(em self-update
Before anything else, the tools check their GitHub remote, fast-forward if
behind, rebuild if a build input changed, restart once, and print the version,
revision and configuration digest in force. The update is fast-forward only: a
dirty or divergent working tree is reported and left alone.
.TP
.B Phase 1 \(em assess
A read-only survey. Gateways first, because everything else is probed through
one; then every node's reachability, logins, disks, mounts, interfaces, link
speeds, services, PCIe state, and the chassis power and event log from its BMC.
No corrective action is taken. Produces the "initial state" pages.
.TP
.B Phase 2 \(em power on
The dependency-ordered power-on sequence, verifying each stage before starting
the next. Dry run unless
.B \-\-execute
is given. Produces the "power on" pages.
.TP
.B Phase 3 \(em network
Node-to-node connectivity across the segments, with a jumbo-frame probe on the
data network. Produces the "network" pages.
.TP
.B Phase 4 \(em report
The consolidated narrative, assembled from the stored run and optionally posted
to the electronic logbook.
.SH STATUS VOCABULARY
.TP
.B OK
The check ran and the answer was right.
.TP
.B WARN
Right enough to proceed, worth an operator's attention.
.TP
.B FAIL
The check ran and the answer was wrong.
.TP
.B UNREACHABLE
The check could not be run, because the node could not be contacted. Reported
separately from FAIL throughout, because "it is broken" and "we could not look"
need different responses during a recovery.
.TP
.B n/a
Not applicable to this host \(em a node with no BMC, a host with no PCIe card.
A node whose every applicable check passed is OK, not n/a.
.SH SAFETY MODEL
.IP \(bu 2
Power actions are a dry run unless
.B \-\-execute
is given; a simulated run cannot be armed at all.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "chassis power off" ", " cycle " and " reset
are refused for the gateways,
.I mu2e-mgr-01
and
.IR mu2e-dcs-01 ,
regardless of flags.
.IP \(bu 2
Every state-changing attempt, including every refusal, is written to the run
store before the command is issued.
.IP \(bu 2
The gateway stage of phase 2 never issues a power command at all.
.IP \(bu 2
A simulated run contacts nothing: the SSH factory, the IPMI client
.I and
the workstation's own local transport are scripted, and the publisher refuses
to copy the report anywhere, reporting
.I "simulated run: the report was written locally but nothing was published."
The publisher needs a refusal of its own because it copies with the filesystem
rather than through a transport, so scripting the transports does not reach it.
.IP \(bu 2
The first IPMI credential rejection stops IPMI for the rest of the run. All the
BMCs share one credential set, so the first refusal settles the matter, and
repeating it only walks the BMC accounts towards lockout; the run reports one
diagnosis naming the username that was refused. Only an RAKP failure or an
.I "unauthorized name"
counts.
.I "Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session"
deliberately does not: that is what a dark chassis says, and after an outage a
dark chassis is the expected case. Set
.I ipmi.stop_on_auth_failure
false to carry on regardless.
.SH REPORTS
Each phase writes a static HTML page and a JSON companion into
.I html/
\(em no server, no build step, readable from a laptop or copied to a web area.
Re-running a phase refreshes its page in place; each run is also archived under
.IR html/runs/<id>/ .
The pages are overview, initial state, power on, network, detailed report, run
history, about, API and site map.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BI MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ PATH
Any configuration key, upper-cased with dots replaced by underscores:
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_SSH_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
sets
.IR ssh.connect_timeout .
Nothing has to be registered for this to work, so a key added to the YAML is
immediately overridable. See
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5).
.TP
.B VAULT_ADDR
Accepted in its conventional form as an alias for
.IR vault.addr ,
because the
.BR vault (1)
CLI and hvac already honour it.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to anything: no ANSI colour, even on a terminal.
.TP
.B FORCE_COLOR
Set to anything: colour even when the output is not a terminal, for a log that
renders escapes.
.B NO_COLOR
wins if both are set.
.TP
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_UPDATED
Set to
.B 1
by phase 0 across the re-exec that follows a self-update, so the restarted
process does not check for updates again. It is documented because it is
visible in the environment of everything the run starts, and because a process
inheriting it skips phase 0 exactly as
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
does \(em which is a surprise if it was not meant. It is not the spelling an
operator should reach for.
.SH CHECK REFERENCE
The checks below are registered by their dotted id and selected by profile in
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5).
Ids marked
.B [root]
need a root session.
.PP
This list is generated from the registry by
.I tools/generate-docs.py
.RB ( mu2edaq-generate-docs (1))
and is verified against it by the test suite, so a check added to the code
without a description here fails the build rather than going unmentioned.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED CHECK REFERENCE
.\" Generated by tools/generate-docs.py -- do not edit by hand.
.TP
.B disk.errors [root]
no storage errors in the kernel log since boot
.TP
.B disk.local
no local filesystem is over its usage threshold
.TP
.B disk.mounts
every expected filesystem is mounted
.TP
.B disk.nfs_from_mgr
shared areas are mounted from mu2e-mgr-01
.TP
.B disk.raid [root]
md/RAID arrays are complete and not rebuilding
.TP
.B disk.smart [root]
SMART overall health for every physical device
.TP
.B host.kernel
kernel release and taint state
.TP
.B host.uptime
uptime and load average, with a post-power-on sanity test
.TP
.B login.users [root]
the mu2edaq and mu2eshift accounts can log in
.TP
.B net.data
the 10 GbE data-network interface is up at link speed
.TP
.B net.dns
forward and reverse name resolution work
.TP
.B net.forwarding [root]
the gateway is routing between the DAQ segments
.TP
.B net.interfaces
every network the node belongs to has an interface up
.TP
.B net.ipmi_reach
the IPMI segment is reachable from this gateway
.TP
.B pcie.devices [root]
the DTC/CFO PCIe cards are enumerated
.TP
.B pcie.driver [root]
the mu2e PCIe driver is loaded with device nodes
.TP
.B ping.lab
ICMP reachability on the lab network, probed from the gateway
.TP
.B power.sel
no new critical entries in the BMC event log
.TP
.B power.sensors
no IPMI sensor is in a critical state
.TP
.B power.status
IPMI chassis power state
.TP
.B ssh.login
an ordinary SSH session can be opened
.TP
.B ssh.login_root [root]
a root SSH session can be opened
.TP
.B svc.firewall [root]
the packet filter is loaded
.TP
.B svc.nfs_export [root]
the NFS exports are published
.TP
.B svc.running
the expected daemons are running
.\" END GENERATED CHECK REFERENCE
